<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Garda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Garda is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, lying at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built villas on its shores, notably at Sirmione, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its towns were contested by Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ruled most of them from the 15th century. In 1943–1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s Italian Social Republic placed its ministries in lakeside towns, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salo">Salò</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-garda.jpg" alt="Lake Garda" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by Guido Adler. Upload by Moroder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Rocca_di_Manerba_del_Garda_con_l%27isola_dei_Conigli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
